WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Suppose this curved roof were made of glass.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.768
How much space is underneath it?

00:00:05.768 --> 00:00:08.026
A formula might tell you the answer.

00:00:08.026 --> 00:00:10.912
But first, let us build an answer you can see.

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:13.399
Choose one small square on the floor.

00:00:13.399 --> 00:00:15.358
Lift it until it touches the roof.

00:00:15.358 --> 00:00:16.684
You have made a column.

00:00:16.684 --> 00:00:18.701
Its height comes from the function.

00:00:18.701 --> 00:00:20.891
Its base comes from the little square.

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:24.540
So this one piece contributes height times base area.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.948
The height changes from place to place.

00:00:26.948 --> 00:00:30.963
That is why counting the squares alone cannot give us the volume.

00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:32.777
Now do that everywhere.

00:00:32.777 --> 00:00:35.600
The columns leave a staircase along the roof.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.298
Add their volumes, and you get an estimate.

00:00:38.298 --> 00:00:41.749
Each term in this sum belongs to one particular column.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.738
Make the squares smaller.

00:00:43.738 --> 00:00:45.966
The staircase begins to disappear.

00:00:45.966 --> 00:00:53.044
Nothing fundamentally new is happening: we are still multiplying a height by an area, and adding the pieces.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.302
The double integral names the number these estimates approach.

00:00:57.302 --> 00:01:02.212
The double sign means our sample points spread across a two dimensional floor.

00:01:02.212 --> 00:01:06.240
The answer is a volume because we chose height as the integrand.

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:11.625
There is a clever way to organize all those columns.

00:01:11.625 --> 00:01:14.119
Keep x fixed, and collect a single row.

00:01:14.119 --> 00:01:18.979
Together, the little columns approximate a vertical slice through the solid.

00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:22.293
Adding along y gives the area of that slice.

00:01:22.293 --> 00:01:25.185
During this first integration, x is frozen.

00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:28.549
The slice area still depends on which x you chose.

00:01:28.899 --> 00:01:31.432
Now slide the slice across the floor.

00:01:31.432 --> 00:01:34.034
Add its area times its tiny thickness.

00:01:34.034 --> 00:01:39.099
The outer integral adds the slices, and the result is the original volume.

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:41.800
We could slice in the other direction.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:47.327
For a continuous function on a rectangle, Fubini says both groupings give the same number.

00:01:47.327 --> 00:01:51.442
We have changed the bookkeeping, while keeping all the same pieces.

00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:58.808
For height x plus y, over this two by one rectangle, the inner integral is x plus one half.

00:01:58.808 --> 00:02:01.966
Integrating from zero to two gives three.

00:02:01.966 --> 00:02:06.895
Read the differentials from the inside outward: first y, then x.

00:02:08.566 --> 00:02:10.953
But a floor need not be a rectangle.

00:02:10.953 --> 00:02:15.528
Here is the region trapped between y equals x squared and y equals x.

00:02:15.528 --> 00:02:19.174
How do we tell the integral which points belong inside?

00:02:19.499 --> 00:02:21.425
Draw one vertical scan line.

00:02:21.425 --> 00:02:25.415
It enters at the parabola and leaves at the straight line.

00:02:25.415 --> 00:02:31.331
As x travels from zero to one, this little strip sweeps the whole region exactly once.

00:02:31.666 --> 00:02:33.735
That drawing gives the bounds.

00:02:33.735 --> 00:02:36.012
The lower edge becomes x squared.

00:02:36.012 --> 00:02:37.736
The upper edge becomes x.

00:02:37.736 --> 00:02:41.530
The outer bounds describe the journey of the scan line.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.186
No guessing is required.

00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:45.722
Now turn the scan line sideways.

00:02:45.722 --> 00:02:50.033
At a fixed height y, the line is the left boundary: x equals y.

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:54.549
The parabola is the right boundary: x equals the square root of y.

00:02:54.866 --> 00:02:58.670
These two integrals describe precisely the same shaded region.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.861
Switching the order means solving for new endpoints.

00:03:01.861 --> 00:03:06.770
It does not mean swapping the differential symbols and leaving the bounds alone.

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:10.308
As a quick check, set the height equal to one.

00:03:10.308 --> 00:03:12.958
Then you are simply adding floor area.

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:16.306
The vertical strip has length x minus x squared.

00:03:16.306 --> 00:03:18.259
The total area is one sixth.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:24.407
For a disk, rectangular strips feel like the wrong building blocks.

00:03:24.407 --> 00:03:27.233
What if we gave our grid rings and spokes?

00:03:27.233 --> 00:03:32.211
Imagine this ordinary coordinate rectangle bending into a circular region.

00:03:32.561 --> 00:03:34.445
Focus on one little tile.

00:03:34.445 --> 00:03:36.555
Its radial thickness is d r.

00:03:36.555 --> 00:03:38.816
Its other edge is a short arc.

00:03:38.816 --> 00:03:42.809
An angle d theta cuts off arc length r times d theta.

00:03:43.128 --> 00:03:46.347
Keep the angle fixed, but push the tile outward.

00:03:46.347 --> 00:03:47.889
Its angular edge grows.

00:03:47.889 --> 00:03:54.192
Twice the radius gives twice the area, even though the coordinate increments have not changed.

00:03:54.528 --> 00:03:56.935
This is where the extra r comes from.

00:03:56.935 --> 00:04:01.359
The physical area of a tiny polar tile is r times d r times d theta.

00:04:01.359 --> 00:04:04.872
The formula records a stretch you just watched happen.

00:04:05.195 --> 00:04:10.940
Now return to a roof: one minus x squared minus y squared, above the unit disk.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:15.377
In polar coordinates, the height becomes one minus r squared.

00:04:15.377 --> 00:04:19.595
The disk becomes a simple rectangle of coordinate choices.

00:04:19.928 --> 00:04:22.005
Multiply height by tile area.

00:04:22.005 --> 00:04:25.874
The radial integral of r minus r cubed is one quarter.

00:04:25.874 --> 00:04:28.238
One full turn contributes two pi.

00:04:28.238 --> 00:04:30.100
The volume is pi over two.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:34.040
The geometry simplified both the bounds and the height.

00:04:35.666 --> 00:04:39.219
The little floor tile could carry something other than height.

00:04:39.219 --> 00:04:41.397
Suppose it carries mass per unit area.

00:04:41.397 --> 00:04:44.090
Here the sheet becomes denser toward the right.

00:04:44.433 --> 00:04:46.995
Density times tile area is a little mass.

00:04:46.995 --> 00:04:50.306
Add those contributions to get the mass of the sheet.

00:04:50.306 --> 00:04:54.993
We have changed what the integrand means, while keeping the same operation.

00:04:55.333 --> 00:04:59.877
To find the balance point, let each little mass vote for its position.

00:04:59.877 --> 00:05:05.005
Weight the x coordinate by mass, add those votes, and divide by the total mass.

00:05:05.005 --> 00:05:07.861
Heavier places pull the average toward them.

00:05:08.199 --> 00:05:12.791
On the unit square with density one plus x, the mass is three halves.

00:05:12.791 --> 00:05:16.585
The balance point is five ninths across, and one half up.

00:05:16.585 --> 00:05:20.511
It shifts right because that is where the extra mass lives.

00:05:20.833 --> 00:05:23.659
The same picture gives other applications.

00:05:23.659 --> 00:05:26.957
Average value divides the weighted total by area.

00:05:26.957 --> 00:05:31.399
A moment of inertia weights mass by squared distance from an axis.

00:05:31.399 --> 00:05:36.985
A probability density assigns probability to each tiny tile; its total must be one.

00:05:39.033 --> 00:05:41.408
Now imagine painting the roof itself.

00:05:41.408 --> 00:05:45.068
The area you paint is larger than the area of its shadow.

00:05:45.068 --> 00:05:49.113
A floor tile and its lifted surface patch have different sizes.

00:05:49.466 --> 00:05:50.862
Watch the patch tilt.

00:05:50.862 --> 00:05:54.054
Its shadow stays fixed, but the patch stretches.

00:05:54.054 --> 00:05:59.906
Close enough to a point, the tangent plane tells us exactly how that stretching behaves.

00:06:00.266 --> 00:06:05.608
Moving one unit in x gives the tangent direction one, zero, f sub x.

00:06:05.608 --> 00:06:09.222
Moving one unit in y gives zero, one, f sub y.

00:06:09.222 --> 00:06:14.642
Their cross product measures the area of the parallelogram they span.

00:06:14.999 --> 00:06:19.330
Its length is the square root of one plus the two squared slopes.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:23.727
Multiply floor area by this local factor, then add over the floor.

00:06:23.727 --> 00:06:27.791
This computes surface area rather than the volume underneath.

00:06:28.166 --> 00:06:31.854
For the plane z equals x plus y, both slopes are one.

00:06:31.854 --> 00:06:35.541
Every patch is square root of three times its shadow.

00:06:35.541 --> 00:06:40.551
Above a unit square, the surface area is therefore square root of three.

00:06:40.551 --> 00:06:43.334
A horizontal roof would have factor one.

00:06:45.399 --> 00:06:49.268
A triple integral begins with a new kind of region.

00:06:49.268 --> 00:06:51.923
Our sample points now fill a solid.

00:06:51.923 --> 00:06:56.703
The tiny pieces are boxes, with volume d x times d y times d z.

00:06:57.066 --> 00:07:02.783
Consider the tetrahedron in the positive octant, under x plus y plus z equals one.

00:07:02.783 --> 00:07:05.572
Choose a point in its triangular shadow.

00:07:05.572 --> 00:07:10.314
A vertical column starts on the floor and stops at the sloping face.

00:07:10.666 --> 00:07:14.113
The roof gives z equals one minus x minus y.

00:07:14.113 --> 00:07:17.561
The shadow gives y from zero to one minus x.

00:07:17.561 --> 00:07:20.381
Finally, x travels from zero to one.

00:07:20.381 --> 00:07:23.986
Read those boundaries from the inside outward.

00:07:24.366 --> 00:07:26.855
Integrate one, and you count volume.

00:07:26.855 --> 00:07:30.450
First the z integral becomes the height of a column.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.768
Then the y integral becomes the area of a slice.

00:07:33.768 --> 00:07:36.534
Finally, the x integral gives one sixth.

00:07:36.899 --> 00:07:41.088
For a round solid, replace the floor grid with polar tiles.

00:07:41.088 --> 00:07:42.650
Stack them vertically.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:45.063
Their volume is r d r d theta d z.

00:07:45.063 --> 00:07:50.459
Cylindrical coordinates are simply polar coordinates with a height attached.

00:07:51.532 --> 00:07:54.633
A ball suggests one more change of coordinates.

00:07:54.633 --> 00:08:03.340
Describe a point by distance rho from the center, angle theta around the vertical axis, and angle phi down from the positive z axis.

00:08:03.699 --> 00:08:06.838
Cut out one small block from a spherical shell.

00:08:06.838 --> 00:08:09.376
There are three lengths to understand.

00:08:09.376 --> 00:08:11.380
Its radial thickness is d rho.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:13.851
Its north to south edge is rho d phi.

00:08:14.232 --> 00:08:17.645
The east to west edge follows a latitude circle.

00:08:17.645 --> 00:08:20.204
That circle has radius rho sine phi.

00:08:20.204 --> 00:08:23.759
So the third edge has length rho sine phi d theta.

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:27.384
Near a pole, the latitude circle almost disappears.

00:08:27.766 --> 00:08:30.023
Multiply the three short lengths.

00:08:30.023 --> 00:08:34.539
The tiny volume is rho squared sine phi times d rho d phi d theta.

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:37.685
The squared radius accounts for larger shells.

00:08:37.685 --> 00:08:41.038
The sine accounts for shrinking latitude circles.

00:08:41.399 --> 00:08:46.707
For a full ball of radius a, the radial integral gives a cubed over three.

00:08:46.707 --> 00:08:48.787
The polar integral gives two.

00:08:48.787 --> 00:08:50.724
The full turn gives two pi.

00:08:50.724 --> 00:08:54.239
Together they recover four pi a cubed over three.

00:08:55.899 --> 00:09:00.383
Rings, spokes, and spherical shells are examples of a larger idea.

00:09:00.383 --> 00:09:03.847
A coordinate change is a machine that bends a grid.

00:09:03.847 --> 00:09:08.331
A unit of coordinate area need not become a unit of physical area.

00:09:08.657 --> 00:09:10.307
Zoom into a tiny square.

00:09:10.307 --> 00:09:14.708
A smooth map turns it into an almost parallelogram shaped patch.

00:09:14.708 --> 00:09:17.252
One edge follows the derivative in u.

00:09:17.252 --> 00:09:19.865
The other follows the derivative in v.

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:25.046
The determinant of those two edge directions gives the signed area scale.

00:09:25.046 --> 00:09:28.239
Take its absolute value to obtain physical area.

00:09:28.239 --> 00:09:33.294
This is the Jacobian: the local exchange rate between the two kinds of area.

00:09:33.623 --> 00:09:38.352
Stretch a unit disk by two horizontally and leave its height alone.

00:09:38.352 --> 00:09:39.693
Every area doubles.

00:09:39.693 --> 00:09:43.293
The disk becomes an ellipse, so its area is two pi.

00:09:43.293 --> 00:09:46.751
A complicated boundary has become a familiar one.

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:53.533
In general, change the region, substitute into the function, and include the absolute Jacobian.

00:09:53.533 --> 00:10:01.874
Use a smooth map that covers the region once, apart from negligible boundaries, with a nonzero determinant in the interior.

00:10:02.223 --> 00:10:05.572
That is the thread through Stewart chapter fifteen.

00:10:05.572 --> 00:10:06.623
Draw the pieces.

00:10:06.623 --> 00:10:08.330
Measure their actual size.

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:10.759
Work out what each piece contributes.

00:10:10.759 --> 00:10:11.350
Then add.

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:16.143
The integral is the total; the geometry tells you what belongs inside it.